reading.summary:
This chapter is a textbook introduction to optimal control through dynamic programming (DP). It is not a research paper and makes no empirical claims.

Framing. Control design is posed as minimizing a scalar long-term cost. Double-integrator examples contrast minimum-time and quadratic-cost formulations. The chapter stresses well-posedness: "the input limits, |u|≤1 are also required to make this problem well-posed; otherwise both optimizations would result in the optimal policy using infinite control input". It derives the bang-bang minimum-time policy and its time-to-go J(x). It notes that J "is continuous over state (though not smooth), even though the policy is discontinuous." It then introduces additive costs ∫ℓ(x,u)dt with finite or infinite horizon, and discrete DP / value iteration on graphs: Ĵ*(s_i) ⇐ min_a[ℓ(s_i,a)+Ĵ*(f(s_i,a))], which "is guaranteed to converge to the optimal cost-to-go up to a constant factor". The policy is extracted as π*(s)=argmin_a[ℓ+J*(f)].

Continuous DP. The Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) equation 0=min_u[ℓ(x,u)+∂J*/∂x f(x,u)] is presented as the continuous analogue of those optimality conditions and as a sufficiency condition. There is an informal "HJB Sufficiency Theorem" for the infinite-horizon, time-invariant case: J is C¹, ℓ is strictly positive definite with a unique zero at x*, J is positive definite, and π is the minimizer, "under some technical conditions on the existence and boundedness of solutions". A more formal version with proof is attributed to A. Megretski. The chapter works a double-integrator LQ example: J=√3q²+2q q̇+√3q̇², u*=−q−√3q̇. It warns that the minimum-time value function violates the C¹ requirement: "Since cusps are commonplace in optimal value functions, one could say that the assumption in the sufficiency theorem that ∂J/∂x is defined everywhere makes our current statement very weak."

Closed-form control. For control-affine dynamics f=f_1(x)+f_2(x)u and cost ℓ_1(x)+uᵀRu with no input constraints, the minimizer has a closed form: u*=−½R⁻¹f_2ᵀ(∂J/∂x)ᵀ. With linear input constraints it becomes a QP. For non-quadratic costs one uses DDP-style quadratic approximations. The rest of the chapter covers:
- representing J* on meshes or neural networks: fitted value iteration, target networks, barycentric interpolation, and the remark that guarantees for generic approximators are "quite weak";
- the time-varying finite-horizon HJB −∂J*/∂t=min_u[ℓ+∂J*/∂x f], solved backward in time on a mesh with "viscosity" terms (Lax-Friedrichs) for numerical robustness;
- discounted cost and its pitfalls, stochastic MDPs, and LP / sums-of-squares DP;
- exercises, including an ill-posed control problem and a discretization exercise showing that the cost-to-go changes with the time step h.

For the proposal, the chapter supplies the formal background for Sec. 10: the HJB equation, the verification (sufficiency) logic, and the closed-form minimizer for control-affine dynamics with quadratic control cost, which directly yields eq. 19. It also provides the caveats that matter most: C¹ differentiability is a strong assumption, value functions commonly have kinks, well-posedness needs extra conditions, and constraints or other control metrics change the minimizer.

PROBLEM. Progress & Compress (P&C) targets continual learning with tasks arriving in sequence. It is framed around five desiderata stated in the Introduction:
(i) no catastrophic forgetting;
(ii) positive forward transfer;
(iii) scalability to many tasks;
(iv) positive backward transfer;
(v) no need for task labels, and "ideally it should even be applicable in the absence of clear task boundaries."
The authors position P&C against three methods: naive fine-tuning (good transfer, forgets), EWC ("the accumulation of Fisher regularisers can over-constrain the network parameters leading to impaired learning of new tasks") and Progressive Nets (no forgetting, but "the network size scales quadratically in the number of tasks").

METHOD. Two networks of fixed size are used: a knowledge base (KB) and an active column (AC). They alternate between a progress ("daytime") phase and a compress ("nighttime") phase. The paper states: "The approach is purposefully reminiscent of daytime and nighttime, and of the role that sleep plays in memory consolidation in humans."
- Progress: the KB is frozen. The AC is trained on the new task "without constraints or regularisation" and reuses KB features through layerwise lateral adaptors taken from Progressive Nets (Eq. 1). The paper recommends re-initialising the AC and adaptors for each new task when tasks are diverse, and keeping them when tasks are very similar.
- Compress: the AC, frozen as the teacher, is distilled into the KB. The loss is the KL/cross-entropy between the teacher policy and the KB policy, plus an "online EWC" penalty that protects earlier KB knowledge (Eq. 2).
- Online EWC applies Laplace's approximation to the whole posterior, as in Huszar 2017. It keeps one anchor (the latest MAP parameters) and one running Fisher, updated as F*_i = gamma F*_{i-1} + F_i. The per-task Fishers are normalised. gamma < 1 gives "graceful" forgetting, and the method needs task boundaries but not task identities.
- The paper claims the method "requires no architecture growth, no access to or storing of previous data or tasks, and no task-specific parameters."

EXPERIMENTS.
- Supervised: 50 Omniglot alphabets learned in sequence, with up to 5 passes.
- RL: 6 Atari games, each visited 5 times for 50M frames per visit, and 8 related 3D navigation mazes. Both use a distributed actor-critic with 4 seeds.
- Results: P&C matches online EWC on retention and avoids EWC's negative transfer. It reaches the best overall Omniglot accuracy among scalable methods (82.84% after 5 passes vs 79.15% for online EWC). On Atari it shows sustained forward transfer only when the AC is re-initialised (125-129% of single-task performance vs about 50-57% for EWC/online EWC). On mazes it matches fine-tuning's forward transfer. Its knowledge base beats EWC/online EWC on several Atari games and is comparable or slightly worse on the others.
- The authors conclude that "P&C is best used in domains that allow for some positive transfer", and that on Omniglot "online EWC on its own is a competitive method."
- Their stated main assumption: "we made the assumption that the learner is aware of when changes in the task distribution occur."

RELATION TO THE PROPOSAL. P&C is a direct precedent for the proposal's fast-to-slow lifecycle (Secs 11-15). The steps line up one to one:
- plastic learner trained with the slow model frozen = fast memory written while theta is fixed;
- behavioural distillation into the slow model under a retention constraint = L_end/L_path + beta R_old;
- re-initialising the plastic learner = resetting W to W_0.
It also shares the sleep/consolidation framing, and its lateral adaptors are a precedent for the proposal's slow-to-fast feature reuse (the query built from h_theta).

What the proposal adds:
- few-shot, meta-learned, episode-local fast weights (1,072 parameters) instead of a full column trained to convergence on each task;
- a centered additive velocity residual, so that reset exactly recovers the base policy;
- readout at every denoising step of a flow-matching policy;
- FM endpoint/path distillation for continuous multimodal actions;
- function-space (velocity) replay retention with a W2 bound, instead of weight-space Fisher protection;
- an explicit accept/reject gate;
- an episodic store for facts that cannot be identified from the current context;
- meta-training of the write/read interfaces across accepted backbone checkpoints.

What the proposal gives up: P&C's defining property of storing no previous data.

ideas_to_borrow:
Add P&C-family arms to the Sec 17 baseline table for Stage D. Sec 13 already calls EWC "a useful alternative comparison", but it is never required. Arms:
(a) online EWC on theta, with FM distillation from the adapted teacher and no replay;
(b) EWC with per-task anchors;
(c) a full P&C instantiation: an adapter/active column trained to convergence on each task with lateral access to theta's features, FM-distilled into theta under online EWC, then re-initialised;
(d) naive sequential fine-tuning of theta.
Report the storage of each arm (online EWC is about 2|theta| floats for anchor plus Fisher) next to the replay bytes.

Borrow the evaluation protocol:
- evaluate the plastic learner (the teacher) for forward transfer and the consolidated slow model (the student) for overall performance, separately;
- normalise by single-task performance ("% single task performance");
- report area under the learning curve for data efficiency;
- run multiple task permutations and multi-pass revisits (P&C: 5 passes);
- use sequences long enough to separate methods (LwF looked fine to about 5 tasks and then collapsed; P&C used 50). Consider 20-50 arrivals for the scalability claim rather than the minimum of 10.

errors_found.problem:
This overreaches for the proposal's setting of 1-16 experiences per context, conditioned on context. DSB needs:
- samples from both marginals;
- L IPF iterations, each training networks on freshly simulated trajectories;
- positive-density marginals (A1).

With a terminal marginal that is an empirical measure of at most 16 demos, an exact SB puts all terminal mass on those demos, which is memorisation. [18]'s consistency statement for empirical marginals holds only as the sample size grows. At best this is an offline or oracle comparison, not a like-for-like few-shot baseline.
